AB  - The integration of Artificial Intelligence (AI) techniques into Software Engineering (SE) has opened new avenues for improving software development, testing, and maintenance processes. This paper explores the transformative potential of AI-driven approaches in addressing some of the most persistent challenges in modern software engineering workflows. Specifically, it highlights the application of AI for automated bug detection and fixing in open-source software projects, which often grapple with complex codebases and evolving requirements. Through a detailed case study based on the Defects4J dataset, we demonstrate how a Transformer-based model can effectively detect bugs and suggest relevant fixes, showcasing an impressive 85% detection rate with a precision of 92%. Additionally, the model successfully proposed accurate fixes for 70% of identified bugs, as verified by developer feedback and performance testing. The integration of this model into Continuous Integration/Continuous Delivery (CI/CD) pipelines further reduced debugging time by 50%, underscoring its practical viability and substantial productivity gains. Moreover, developer feedback emphasized the system's usability, with many praising its ability to identify subtle bugs that often escape manual detection. However, challenges persist, including false positives and handling highly intricate bug scenarios, which remain areas for future refinement. This study not only underscores the efficacy of AI tools in augmenting traditional SE practices but also paves the way for future innovations in hybrid AI-human debugging workflows. By combining advanced machine learning architectures with real-world applicability, this research bridges the gap between theory and practice, offering a scalable solution for improving software reliability and developer efficiency.
ER  - 

