AB  - This paper provides a survey on the latest developments of tools for Web application security with a comprehensive assessment of eight published studies from 2020 to 2025 – insight into their efficiency, how much they are integrated into artificial intelligence (AI), and regulations such as PCI DSS/GDPR. Improvements in the detection of well-known flaws are evident, as demonstrated by Burp Suite reaching 70%+ coverage of OWASP Top 10 risks and OWASP ZAP with the best cost-effective scanning at this post from a set of tools including AI-driven frameworks such as the Intelligent Automated Penetration Testing System (IAPTS). The MDDQN (relational) algorithm shows promising results in few-shot adversarial robustness via acceleration of attack path planning from reinforcement learning and deep neural networks for efficiency. Still, some are quite challenging, e.g., high computational costs, misidentifications, and inability to detect zero-days necessitate the motivations for hybrid approaches combining automated and manual testing. Reviewed studies stress the critical need for evolving threats tackled by multi-tool strategies and integrations in continues integration/continuous delivery (CI/CD) pipelines for ongoing testing. Regulatory compliance is important, so tools to allow the regular upgrades of compliance will be useful. Investigation directions should focus on establishing benchmarks, lowering the barriers to implementation of AI, and improving detection of advanced attacks. Through synthesis of technical developments and practitioner experiences, this research aimed to improve the comprehension of automated penetration testing capabilities in securing web apps from evolving cyberattacks as of 2025.
ER  - 

