AB  - Modern continuous integration / continuous delivery (CI/CD) pipelines run security tests—static analysis, dynamic fuzzing, policy checks—on production-like data long before code reaches production. This shift-left practice speeds feedback but exposes sensitive records to pre-production tools. Existing redaction and tokenisation often degrade test accuracy and provide no formal privacy guarantee, leaving DevSecOps teams torn between realism and compliance. We present a drop-in framework that delivers differentially private (DP) snapshots to every pipeline stage without slowing release cadence. Key features are: (i) multi-level privacy ledgers (user, build, organization); (ii) calibrated Laplace and Gaussian mechanisms plus a deep-Q-network scheduler that learns to allocate the per-query privacy budget; and (iii) a policy-as-code interface that lets builds request, reserve, and audit budget on demand. Formal analysis shows the design retains DP under arbitrary post-processing and subsampling. On representative payroll and healthcare workloads (up to 107 rows) the framework keeps aggregate error below two percent with a budget of ε = 1, adds only 8.7 percent runtime overhead, and sustains about 1400 builds per day—nearly doubling throughput versus strong-composition baselines. Accuracy improves by 40–110% compared with the best published sanitisation scheme. These results confirm that strong mathematical privacy, machine-learning budget control, and near-real-time DevSecOps automation can co-exist, enabling auditable security testing in highly regulated environments without sacrificing data utility.
ER  - 

