AB  - In today's fast-paced software development landscape, DevOps appeared as a transformative methodology that highlights speed, collaboration, and continuous delivery. However, integrating better security protocols into agile environments is persistent challenge for many teams. Even though DevSecOps builds upon DevOps by weaving security measures throughput every step of development companies finds it difficult to move quickly while still adhering to strict compliance, governance and risk requirements. This paper gives an in-depth look at different DevOps frameworks that prioritize security with each one undertaking specific part of the delivery process. To make sense of these different methods, paper organizes them into seven different categories based on optimization those driven by Machine Learning (ML), block chain-integrated systems models backed by experiential testing governance focused techniques, context-specific alignments and general conceptual frameworks. Drawing from both academic and industry sources, the paper analyzes how each method tackles issues such as anomaly detection, secure deployment, auditability, and cultural transformation. It highlights recurring challenges like fragmented governance, delayed security integration, and lack of real-time adaptability. By organizing these frameworks into a structured taxonomy, the paper gives researchers and experts a combined reference for understanding and implementing resilient, scalable, and secure DevOps pipelines across diverse organizational contexts, while emphasizing the need for empirically validated, context-aware results.
ER  - 

